    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> There Are So Many Types of Clubs!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Extract key factual information, such as dates, numbers, and quantities from a presentation. Interactive: Interpret and relay information in pair and group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can extract detailed factual information from conversations and presentations about club-related tasks, such as goals, timelines, and membership requirements (e.g., "What are the club's main objectives?" "When is the registration deadline?" "How many members are needed for the environmental clean-up event?").
                Can interpret and relay key details about different club activities from discussions based on a presentation or lecture (e.g., "The types of clubs mentioned are the social, sports, and academic clubs.").
                Can follow a presentation on club organization and answer specific questions (e.g., "What date is the club fair?" "The club fair is scheduled for next Thursday.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE</h2>
<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of school clubs (e.g., a chess club, a sports team, a science fair project, a community garden event). Students listen to the teacher say the name of each club and identify it from the pictures. For example, "Listen: This is a *sports club*." "This group is organizing a *community event*." The teacher asks, "What type of *club* do you see?" to activate prior knowledge. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary orally, writing it on the board: *clubs, interests, activities, goals, members, requirements*. The teacher models the pronunciation, specifically focusing on the /pr/ sound in "project" and the stress on the third syllable from the end in verbs like "coordinate" and "participate." The teacher says, "Listen carefully: We will *coordinate* our *project*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a club has 20 *members*, is that one person or many people? (Many people)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If I say a club has *goals*, does it mean it has a plan or no plan? (A plan)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students listen to an audio recording of a school administrator giving a presentation about the upcoming "Annual Club Fair" and explaining how students can *organize* or *join* various *clubs*. The audio includes details about different types of clubs, *membership requirements*, *activity* examples, and key dates. (Genre: school announcement/presentation, Register: informative, Format: audio recording).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets and relays key information from an oral presentation on club organization and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the presentation and identify the main purpose: "What is the speaker mainly talking about?" (Club Fair and starting clubs).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Detailed information):</b> Students listen again and complete a simple table or answer True/False questions based on factual information heard.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "The registration deadline is next Tuesday." / "The Art Club has 15 *members*."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Fill-in-the-blank: "The Science Club’s main *goal* is to ___." (e.g., "conduct *projects*"). "The club fair is scheduled for next _____."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Question: "How many *members* are needed to *create* a new club?" (e.g., "Minimum 5 *members*.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                The teacher focuses on students extracting specific details like dates, numbers, and types of activities. Grammar focus: Simple present ("There are many types of clubs.") and target vocabulary like *schedule*, *organize*, *participate*.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for and relaying factual information using simple present, imperatives, and target vocabulary (*clubs, interests, activities, goals, members, requirements, structure, responsibilities, meetings, team, project, event, participate, organize, schedule, arrange*). Pragmatic function: Asking about club preferences. Pronunciation: Contrastive sounds /p/ and /t/, stress on "-ate" verbs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening for specifics):</b> The teacher plays short audio snippets (20-30 seconds each) describing different fictional clubs. For each snippet, students listen and fill in a "Club Fact Sheet" with specific details: Club Name, Main *Goal*, Key *Activities*, Number of *Members* mentioned, Meeting Day/Time. E.g., "The Eco-Warriors Club *organizes* *community* clean-up *events* every Saturday." "Their main *goal* is to protect the environment."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and relay):</b> In pairs, students listen to audio descriptions of two different clubs (e.g., a "Debate Club" and a "Coding Club"). After listening, Student A relays information about the first club to Student B, and Student B relays information about the second club to Student A. They use phrases like: "The speaker mentioned that the Debate Club's *goals* are..." or "I heard they *schedule* their *meetings* on Thursdays." Teacher encourages pronunciation of /p/ in "participate" and /t/ in "team."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Asking for preferences):</b> Students listen to five short sentences, each describing a different student's *interest* (e.g., "I like solving puzzles." "I enjoy playing sports."). In pairs, they then ask each other: "What type of *club* would you like to join based on what you *heard*?" or "Which *activities* did you hear that match your *interests*?" Student A asks, Student B listens and responds. They focus on using simple present to describe preferences: "I like this club because we can *plan activities* together."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Club Pitch & Q&A Simulation." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group will imagine they are presenting a new club idea to a school committee. One student acts as the "presenter" and verbally pitches their club idea for 1-2 minutes, detailing its *goals*, *activities*, *membership requirements*, and how they plan to *organize* it. The other students in the group act as "committee members" and listen actively, taking notes. After the pitch, committee members ask specific questions based on what they heard, such as "What are the club's main *objectives*?" "When is the *registration deadline* for *members*?" "How many *members* are needed for this *project*?" The presenter responds. Groups rotate roles. This promotes listening for details and asking clarifying questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Directly aligns with "Let's Start a Club!" and "There Are So Many Types of Clubs!" by having students articulate and listen to club proposals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in understanding and relaying listened information, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students listen to identify key information and ask follow-up questions. They practice using simple present to describe clubs, simple past to narrate (e.g., "Last year, I joined a sports club and it was great!"), and imperatives (e.g., "Set clear *goals*!"). They also practice negotiating roles implicitly through the Q&A, and responding to feedback/suggestions heard from peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist. The teacher uses a checklist to observe students' ability to extract key factual information during the "Club Pitch & Q&A Simulation," specifically noting if they ask questions that demonstrate comprehension of details like dates, numbers, *goals*, and *requirements*. The teacher also notes their ability to relay information heard accurately during group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students listen to a final short audio message (e.g., a voicemail from a club leader) about an upcoming *event* or *meeting*. They then write down 3-4 key factual details they *heard*: (e.g., "Name of club:", "Date of *event*:", "Number of required *participants*:", "What to *bring* (if mentioned)").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible identification of key factual information from the audio (dates, numbers, main *goals*, *requirements*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (*clubs, interests, activities, goals, members, requirements, project, event*) when relaying or writing information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in interpreting and relaying information heard in pair and group discussions.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two questions on an exit slip:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words or phrases did you *hear* today about *clubs* that you found useful?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How confident are you now in *listening* for specific details about *club activities*? (1-5 scale)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the most challenging part of *listening* to the club descriptions today, and how did you overcome it?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can *listening* for specific details help you in real-life situations when joining a new group or *project*?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The focus on listening for factual details (dates, numbers, *requirements*) worked well, especially with the use of short audio snippets and explicit tasks. Students showed good engagement in identifying the types of *clubs* and their *goals*. Some students struggled with differentiating between similar-sounding numbers or dates; I need to provide more scaffolded practice with numerical dictation. For the next lesson, I will ensure visual aids accompany more challenging audio input to support comprehension and transition smoothly to reading the same vocabulary in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and focused on listening for detailed information about different types of *clubs* and their *activities*. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build on this by having students read texts and descriptions of various *clubs* using the same vocabulary, allowing them to see the words in written context and further solidify their understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>